<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/foundational/walk-through-western-art">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Art History. You need zero prior knowledge — no painting
    experience, no memorised dates, no jargon. If you have ever looked at a picture, you
    are ready.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the five questions art historians ask of any artwork, in plain words;</li>
    <li>walk through six centuries of Western art, from Giotto (1305) to Warhol (1962), and say what changed and why;</li>
    <li>explain how new materials — oil paint, paint tubes, the camera — changed what artists could do;</li>
    <li>say who paid for art in each era, and why that often explains the picture;</li>
    <li>apply the five questions yourself to two paintings made three centuries apart.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every new term is explained the moment it appears. Take your time — one idea per
    paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a strange fact</h2>
  <p>
    Portraits made a few centuries apart can show the same kind of thing — a person, a
    saint, a bowl of fruit — and still feel like they come from different worlds. One is
    flat and gold. One is lit like a film still. One is a stack of soup cans in a gallery.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why? That question <em>is</em> art history. The subject is not a parade of genius
    portraits. It is the record of what different eras <em>needed images to do</em>: teach
    the illiterate, glorify the patrons who paid, see accurately, express inner feeling,
    question the art market itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    To answer it, art historians ask the same five questions of every work. Think of them
    as a checklist for meeting a stranger: where did they grow up, who did they know, what
    are they made of, what work did they do, and what have people said about them since?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cultural context</strong> — what was happening in that society when the work was made.</li>
    <li><strong>Interactions across cultures</strong> — what ideas, objects and materials travelled from far away to make it possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Materials and techniques</strong> — what the object is physically made of, and what that making made possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose and audience</strong> — who paid for it, who looked at it, and what it was for.</li>
    <li><strong>Theories and interpretations</strong> — what the work has meant to people since.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The walk ahead passes eleven works. Scrub through the timeline below.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="six centuries, eleven works — scrub through the shifts" widget="timeline" config={artTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> scrub the timeline end to end and watch the spacing
    of the works themselves — nearly two centuries between Giotto and Leonardo, then a
    rapid-fire run from Impressionism onward, roughly one overturning per generation after
    1872. Read each event's tag and watch the labels accumulate: renaissance roots,
    baroque, impressionism, modern. The lesson's five questions are the tools for
    explaining every jump — what the era needed images to do, what materials arrived, and
    who paid.
  </p>

  <h2>Cultural context: what images were for</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cultural context</strong> means the world around the artwork: the beliefs,
    money and events that surrounded its making. Start where the timeline starts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Giotto painted the Arena Chapel in Padua around 1305. In his world, <em>images were the
    church's literacy</em>. Most viewers could not read, so Bible stories had to make sense
    at a glance. What Giotto added was weight: figures with volume, gestures with feeling,
    a space the eye could walk into. It was the first step out of flat medieval convention
    toward observed humanity.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Renaissance followed — the word means "rebirth": a rebirth of interest in the art
    and ideas of ancient Greece and Rome. Leonardo began the <em>Mona Lisa</em> in 1503.
    Michelangelo unveiled the Sistine ceiling in 1512: over 5,000 square feet of vault,
    painted mostly by one man on scaffolding he designed. On that ceiling, the body itself
    becomes theology. Behind the art stood <strong>humanism</strong> — a way of thinking
    that put the human person, not only heaven, at the centre of attention — plus wealthy
    courts and church offices paying the bills.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then came the <strong>Counter-Reformation</strong> — the Catholic Church's campaign to
    win hearts back after the Reformation split it. It demanded that art persuade. In 1563
    the Council of Trent ruled that religious images should teach clearly and move the
    viewer. Caravaggio's answer, around 1600, was the <strong>Baroque</strong> style:
    violent spotlights, ordinary models, saints with dirty feet. Realism as recruitment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Meanwhile the Dutch Republic invented a new audience. No king, no church commission —
    just merchants buying pictures for their homes. That is why Rembrandt's <em>Night
    Watch</em> (1642) is a militia group portrait turned into moving theatre: light as
    storytelling, made for a paying civic client rather than an altar.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> images had jobs — teaching, glorifying, persuading,
    decorating — and each job shaped what the art looked like. Next: where the ingredients
    came from.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions: what travelled to make the art possible</h2>
  <p>
    The second question, <strong>interactions across cultures</strong>, asks what entered
    the work from the outside. No work on this timeline is culturally self-made. The
    materials travelled first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take ultramarine, the Renaissance's most precious blue. It was ground from lapis
    lazuli, a stone mined in a single Afghan valley and hauled overland to Venice. It cost
    more than gold — which is why painters saved it for the Virgin's robes. Patronage
    written in pigment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Oil painting itself — perfected by Jan van Eyck in Flanders in the early 1400s —
    spread along trade routes into Italy within a generation. Ideas travelled too.
    <strong>Japonisme</strong> was the European passion for Japanese woodblock prints,
    which arrived after Japan opened its ports in the 1850s. Their flat colour, cropped
    views and expressive line reset European composition. Monet owned dozens of prints by
    Hokusai and Hiroshige, and Van Gogh copied them openly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The starkest case is Picasso's <em>Les Demoiselles d'Avignon</em> (1907). Its shattered
    planes and mask-like faces owe a documented debt to African and Iberian carvings —
    Picasso had seen African masks at the Trocadéro. Modern scholarship examines that
    borrowing from both sides: as the spark of Cubism, and as Europe consuming other
    cultures' sacred objects as raw material. The colonial dimension runs through the whole
    story, from the silver of Potosí funding Spanish churches to Latin American modernists
    — Rivera, Kahlo, Lam — reworking European painting with Indigenous and Afro-Atlantic
    content. Interactions are not a footnote. They are how the objects got made.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> oil painting was an Italian Renaissance invention. The medium was perfected a century earlier in Flanders — Jan van Eyck's workshop in the 1420s–30s — and travelled south along trade routes within a generation; Italian painters adopted it because tempera's drying speed made sfumato and luminous glaze-building impossible. Technique, like ultramarine, belongs to the story of interactions across cultures.</p>

  <h2>Materials and techniques: the medium is an idea</h2>
  <p>
    The third question asks what the work is made of. The <strong>medium</strong> is the
    physical stuff — paint, plaster, bronze — plus the method of working it. Every
    stylistic shift above had a technical enabler.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Egg tempera</strong>, the medieval standard, mixes pigment with egg yolk. It
    dries in minutes — fine for crisp icons, hopeless for subtlety. <strong>Oil
    paint</strong> dries slowly and can be reworked, which invented the painter's ability
    to revise. Painters built <strong>glazes</strong> — thin transparent layers — so light
    passes through and glows back out. The Mona Lisa's <strong>sfumato</strong> (Italian
    for "smoke") — edges dissolved into haze — is dozens of such veils, technically
    impossible in tempera.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fresco</strong> means painting pigment into wet plaster. It gave Giotto and
    Michelangelo permanence — and a hard deadline. Each <em>giornata</em> was one day's
    plaster, one day's painting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 19th century industrialised the materials. <strong>Paint in collapsible
    tubes</strong> (John Goffe Rand, 1841) took painting out of the studio — which is why
    Impressionism could exist. Monet's <em>Impression, Sunrise</em> (1872) was painted at a
    harbour window in minutes. A critic, Louis Leroy, mocked the group with the title
    "Impressionists". The insult became the movement's name.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Photography</strong>, announced in 1839, was the century's structural event: it
    took likeness away from painting. Much of the modern half of the timeline is a
    150-year answer. Impressionism chased the eye's instant. Van Gogh's <em>Starry
    Night</em> (1889) made paint into feeling — sky in visible strokes,
    <strong>impasto</strong> (paint laid on thick) as emotion. Cubism showed all sides of a
    thing at once, something a camera cannot do. Pollock's drips (1947) put the canvas on
    the floor and recorded the artist's movement directly. And Duchamp's
    <strong>readymade</strong> — an ordinary manufactured object the artist selects and
    signs — <em>Fountain</em> (1917), a signed urinal, moved art-making from craft to
    selection: a technique of ideas.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> tempera forced crispness, oil allowed revision, fresco set a
    deadline, tubes freed painting from the studio, and the camera pushed painting to ask
    what only painting could do. Materials are ideas.
  </p>

  <h2>Purpose and audience: who paid, who looked</h2>
  <p>
    The fourth question follows the money. A <strong>patron</strong> is the person or
    institution that pays for a work. The chain of patronage is the economic spine of our
    walk: <strong>church → court → merchant → museum → market</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Medieval and Renaissance art answered to altarpieces and princes. Caravaggio answered
    to church rectors with budgets and tastes — his realism was commissioned, not merely
    rebelled. Rembrandt answered to a bourgeois art market the Netherlands invented: the
    first era when ordinary paintings were ordinary commodities.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 19th century added the <strong>Salon system</strong> — the French state exhibition
    whose jury could make or destroy a career. The Impressionists answered with
    institutional revolt: in 1874 they staged their own independent exhibition outside the
    Salon, the prototype of the artist-run gallery.
  </p>
  <p>
    The 20th century completed the shift to the museum and the market. Duchamp's urinal was
    submitted to (and rejected by) an exhibition that claimed to accept anything — that was
    the point, because the artwork's frame had become the institution itself. Warhol's
    <em>Campbell's Soup Cans</em> (1962) put the supermarket shelf in the gallery and asked
    what the difference between consumption and culture really is, in an art world now run
    by dealers, critics and museums rather than popes. Reading the patron usually explains
    the picture faster than reading the style.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Rembrandt's <em>Night Watch</em> depicts a night scene. It is a daylight militia setting out; layers of darkened varnish, accumulated over centuries, created the nocturnal mood that fixed the nickname, and later cleaning revealed the daylight that had always been there. The picture is a standing lesson in the page's method: read the object's physical history before you read the image.</p>

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: how to look</h2>
  <p>
    The last question is about looking itself. Interpretation is a method, not a mood. The
    working sequence: <em>What do I literally see?</em> (value, colour, space — the formal
    layer) → <em>How was it made?</em> → <em>Who was it for?</em> → <em>What did it
    change?</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    The formal layer has its own traditions — Wölfflin's paired concepts, such as linear
    versus painterly and flat versus deep. The meaning layer has
    <strong>iconography</strong>: Erwin Panofsky's method of reading subjects and symbols.
    The lily means purity; the skull means mortality.
  </p>
  <p>
    Since the 1970s this layer has been critical too. Feminist art history — Linda
    Nochlin's 1971 question "Why have there been no great women artists?", and the answer:
    the institutions, not the talent — and the Guerrilla Girls' audits of museum walls have
    made <em>who is represented and who is omitted</em> a mainstream question. Even the
    <strong>canon</strong> — the list of works the art world treats as important — is now
    an object of study. The eleven works in this lesson were chosen by centuries of
    collectors, museums and textbooks, Gombrich's <em>The Story of Art</em> among them.
    Knowing the canon is constructed is not cynicism. It is permission to keep asking what
    the construction left out.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> art history is not a story of progress toward realism, and the
  Renaissance did not "discover" skill that the Middle Ages lacked. Medieval abstraction was a
  choice serving different purposes (symbols, hierarchy, heaven's geometry) — Giotto's naturalism
  answered a new need, not a new ability. Judging every era by its distance from photographic
  accuracy is the anachronism that the post-1839 half of the timeline exists to cure.</p>

  <h2>The method applied: two works, three centuries apart</h2>
  <p>
    Watch the questions do real work on a pairing: Giotto's <em>Lamentation</em> in the
    Arena Chapel (around 1305) against Caravaggio's <em>The Entombment of Christ</em>
    (around 1602–04). Same subject family — mourning a dead Christ. Different worlds.
  </p>
  <p>
    What do you literally see? Giotto: a shallow stage, figures in simplified volumes,
    grief expressed through a choreography of bowed backs and outflung hands, angels
    screaming in a patterned sky. Caravaggio: a single raking light, bodies half-swallowed
    by darkness, the dead Christ's arm hanging over the tomb edge into the viewer's space.
  </p>
  <p>
    How was it made? Giotto worked in fresco on wet plaster — committed in a day, colour
    fixed into the wall. Caravaggio worked in oil on canvas — correctable, glazeable,
    capable of the spotlight gradients fresco cannot hold.
  </p>
  <p>
    Who was it for? Giotto painted a private funerary chapel for a Paduan moneylender,
    Enrico Scrovegni — buying prayer against the sin of usury. Caravaggio painted a Roman
    church altarpiece for the Counter-Reformation's campaign to make faith felt. And what
    changed? Giotto reopened the door to observed nature that the Trecento — Italy's
    fourteenth century — walked through for a hundred years. Caravaggio's naturalism and
    light became the Baroque and spread across Europe through his followers. Same religion,
    same grief — but the context, the technique, the patron and the influence chain are
    entirely different. Saying so precisely is what an art-historical argument <em>is</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> five questions — context, interactions, materials, audience,
    interpretation — turn two paintings of the same subject into two different worlds. Try
    them on any picture this week.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the five questions art historians ask of any work.</li>
    <li>Why could Monet paint a harbour scene in minutes in 1872, while a medieval painter could not have worked that way?</li>
    <li>A painter saves his most expensive blue for one figure's robe. What does that tell you about the money and the meaning behind the work?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Cultural context, interactions across cultures, materials
    and techniques, purpose and audience, and theories and interpretations. (2) Paint had
    only just come in collapsible tubes (1841), freeing painters to work outdoors and fast;
    tempera and fresco forced slower, studio-bound rhythms. (3) The blue is ultramarine,
    ground from lapis lazuli hauled from Afghanistan and costing more than gold — so the
    robe displays both the patron's wealth and the figure's importance.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Art history is the record of what different eras needed images to do — not a parade of genius portraits.</li>
    <li>Five questions unlock any work: cultural context, interactions across cultures, materials and techniques, purpose and audience, theories and interpretations.</li>
    <li>Every stylistic shift had a technical enabler — oil allowed revision, fresco set a deadline, paint tubes freed the studio, the camera took likeness away.</li>
    <li>The patronage chain — church, court, merchant, museum, market — usually explains the picture faster than the style does.</li>
    <li>Materials and ideas travelled: ultramarine from Afghanistan, oil paint from Flanders, Japanese prints into Europe, African carvings into Picasso's studio.</li>
    <li>Interpretation is a method, not a mood: what you literally see, how it was made, who it was for, what it changed.</li>
    <li>The canon is constructed — knowing that is permission to keep asking what the construction left out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Later lessons slow the walk down — individual periods and artists in depth, and the
    non-Western traditions this Western-focused first pass could only gesture toward. The
    deeper section below completes the method with three patterns: periodisation as
    convenience, the moving centres, and the questions that never closed.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper patterns complete the walk. <strong>Periodisation is a convenience, not a
    truth.</strong> "Renaissance", "Baroque" and "modern" are sorting labels invented
    between the 18th and 20th centuries, and their edges are fuzzy. Caravaggio was painting
    while Shakespeare wrote, and the "modern" break had precedents in Goya's black
    paintings decades before the Impressionists. The labels are useful scaffolding — and
    artefacts of the view from here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The centres move.</strong> The timeline's geography drifts: Italy, then the
    Dutch Republic, then Paris, then New York replacing Paris after the Second World War —
    Pollock's drips in 1947 were the moment the art capital crossed the Atlantic. Each move
    tracks money, war and institutions as much as talent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The questions keep extending.</strong> Duchamp's institutional question — is
    art what museums say it is? — has never closed. It runs through conceptual art, land
    art and today's debates about AI images (whose selection? whose training data?). And
    the modern conversation about what to do once photography took likeness away is still
    running: every new reproductive technology re-asks what an image is <em>for</em>. That
    is the deepest use of the method. It works on any object in any century, because it
    asks what the culture needed, what travelled, what the hand did, who paid, and what
    changed — five questions, eleven works, six centuries, one discipline.
  </p>
